---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/ifrspublications/managementcommentary.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Purpose & Status" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Objective of Management Commentary" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Six Content Elements" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Business Model" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Strategy & Resource Allocation" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Resources, Relationships & Material Risks" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Performance & Prospects" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Guiding Principles – Materiality & Connectivity" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Guiding Principles – Forward-Looking & Balanced" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Worked Example – Applying Connectivity" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship with Sustainability Disclosure" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Management Commentary vs US MD&A" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Purpose & Status</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A voluntary framework, not a Standard</div>
        <div class="principle">This Practice Statement provides a broad, NON-MANDATORY framework for the presentation of management commentary that relates to financial statements prepared in accordance with IFRS Standards. Entities are NOT required to comply with it unless a jurisdiction specifically mandates its use – it exists to promote consistency and quality where management commentary IS prepared, whether voluntarily or under local regulatory requirements.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The 2021 revision – broader than the 2010 original</div>
        <div class="principle">The original 2010 Practice Statement focused narrowly on explaining financial statement figures. The 2021 revision substantially broadened the framework to address a company's ability to create and preserve VALUE over time – including its use of, and dependence on, a wider range of RESOURCES and RELATIONSHIPS (not just financial capital), reflecting growing investor interest in a more holistic view of company performance and prospects.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Who this framework is aimed at</div>
        <div class="principle">The Practice Statement is intended for entities that prepare financial statements in accordance with IFRS Standards and choose (or are required by a jurisdiction) to accompany those statements with a narrative management commentary – often referred to as a "management discussion and analysis," "operating and financial review," or "strategic report" in various jurisdictions' own regulatory terminology.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Objective of Management Commentary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Providing context the financial statements alone cannot</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Management commentary should provide primary users of general purpose financial statements with MANAGEMENT'S VIEW of the entity's performance, position, and progress, including insight into: the entity's business model; its STRATEGY for sustaining and developing that business model; the RESOURCES and RELATIONSHIPS the entity depends on; material RISKS and how they are managed; and its performance and PROSPECTS – connecting this narrative directly to the amounts recognised and disclosed in the financial statements.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Six Content Elements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The framework's core content structure</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-03-27"><span>#</span><span>Content element</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-03-27"><span>1</span><span>Business model</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-03-27"><span>2</span><span>Strategy for sustaining and developing that business model</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-03-27"><span>3</span><span>Resources, and relationships with stakeholders, on which the entity depends</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-03-27"><span>4</span><span>Risks that could substantively affect the entity's business model, resources, or relationships</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-03-27"><span>5</span><span>External environment in which the entity operates</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-03-27"><span>6</span><span>Performance and prospects, including progress toward strategic objectives</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Business Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">How the entity creates and preserves value</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">A description of an entity's BUSINESS MODEL explains how the entity generates and preserves value over time – the inputs it uses, the business ACTIVITIES it undertakes to transform those inputs, and the OUTPUTS and OUTCOMES those activities produce. It provides the essential context within which users interpret every other element of the commentary, and against which the financial statements themselves should be read.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Strategy & Resource Allocation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Not just aspiration – connected to actual decisions</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Commentary on STRATEGY should explain management's plans for sustaining and developing the business model, including how the entity intends to allocate resources to achieve its objectives – connecting stated strategic priorities to concrete evidence of resource allocation decisions actually taken (e.g. capital expenditure, R&D investment, acquisitions), rather than remaining purely aspirational or generic.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Resources, Relationships & Material Risks</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Beyond financial capital</div>
        <div class="principle">The 2021 framework explicitly recognises that entities depend on a BROADER range of resources and relationships than financial capital alone – including, where material, human capital (workforce), natural capital (environmental resources), social capital (customer/community relationships), and intellectual capital – and expects commentary to address dependence on these where material to understanding value creation.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Material risks, connected to the business model</div>
        <div class="principle">Risk disclosure should be entity-specific and connected directly to how identified risks could affect the business model, resources, or relationships described elsewhere in the commentary – avoiding generic, boilerplate risk factor lists disconnected from the entity's actual circumstances.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Performance & Prospects</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Backward-looking results, forward-looking outlook</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">This element addresses BOTH historical performance (progress against previously stated objectives, key performance indicators relevant to the specific entity) AND forward-looking PROSPECTS – management's assessment of the entity's likely future development, connecting back to the strategy and risks described elsewhere, so a reader can assess the credibility and coherence of management's overall narrative.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Guiding Principles – Materiality & Connectivity</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Two of the framework's core principles</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">MATERIALITY</div>
            <div class="text-supporting">Management commentary should disclose information necessary for primary users to assess the entity's prospects and management's stewardship – using the SAME concept of materiality that applies to the financial statements themselves, entity-specific rather than a fixed checklist.</div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">CONNECTIVITY</div>
            <div class="text-supporting">Information within the commentary, and between the commentary and the financial statements, should be presented in a way that shows the RELATIONSHIPS between pieces of information – e.g. connecting a stated risk to its actual financial statement impact, or a strategic initiative to the resulting capital expenditure.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Guiding Principles – Forward-Looking & Balanced</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The remaining core principles</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Provides management's VIEW, not merely a restatement of financial statement facts</span>
          <span>Supplements, and is CONSISTENT WITH, the financial statements – not a substitute for them</span>
          <span>FORWARD-LOOKING, including credible information about future prospects, not solely historical</span>
          <span>Fair, BALANCED, and understandable – favourable and unfavourable aspects presented even-handedly</span>
          <span>Prepared and presented on a basis consistent over TIME, supporting comparability</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example – Applying Connectivity</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Linking a stated risk to its financial statement impact</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: A raw material price risk, connected through to the numbers</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A manufacturing entity's risk section identifies exposure to volatile copper prices as a material risk to its business model.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Applying CONNECTIVITY, rather than leaving this as an isolated risk statement, the commentary would link it to: the entity's hedging strategy (linking to a note disclosure on derivative financial instruments), the actual gross margin impact experienced during the period (linking to the SOPL), and management's stated mitigation plans going forward (linking to the strategy section) – presenting the risk as part of one coherent, cross-referenced narrative rather than a standalone disclosure.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship with Sustainability Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">A complementary, but distinct, framework</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Since the establishment of the International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB) in 2021, entities reporting under IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards (e.g. IFRS S1, IFRS S2) produce sustainability-related financial disclosures ALONGSIDE – and are expected to connect with – their general purpose financial statements. Management commentary prepared under this Practice Statement is a NATURAL location for that connectivity, though the ISSB Standards themselves are separate, mandatory-where-adopted pronouncements distinct from this non-mandatory Practice Statement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Management Commentary vs US MD&A</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IFRS Management Commentary vs SEC Management's Discussion and Analysis (MD&A)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRS Management Commentary</span><span>US MD&A (SEC Regulation S-K)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Mandatory status</span><span>Voluntary framework – mandatory only if a jurisdiction separately requires it</span><span>MANDATORY for US public company SEC filings, with specific prescribed content items</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Content structure</span><span>Six broad content elements, applied with judgement and materiality</span><span>More PRESCRIPTIVE regulatory content requirements specified by SEC rule</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Broader capital/resource focus</span><span>Explicit focus on human, natural, social, and intellectual capital in the 2021 revision</span><span>Less explicit; broader ESG-type disclosure in the US has developed through SEPARATE, evolving SEC rulemaking</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The two frameworks share a common purpose – narrative context for financial statements – but differ sharply in binding force: IFRS Management Commentary is a voluntary framework, while US MD&A is a mandatory, closely regulated filing requirement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Connectivity", definition: "A guiding principle requiring information within the commentary, and between the commentary and the financial statements, to show the relationships between related pieces of information." },
      { term: "Business model", definition: "How an entity generates and preserves value over time – its inputs, business activities, and the outputs/outcomes those activities produce." },
      { term: "Primary users", definition: "Existing and potential investors, lenders, and other creditors – the same primary user group the Conceptual Framework identifies for the financial statements themselves." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ManagementCommentary} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>



